From 7fd3eda44ad6cc997e81ed9a83c12770e7ef9ed8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marco Onorati <m.onorati@web.de>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 11:15:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] allwinner: h713: our own secure payload at S-EL1 - kept
 as evidence, not shipped

Before the EL3 route worked, the plan was a payload of our own at S-EL1: TF-A's own
Test Secure Payload with our HDCP code in it, entered through the TSP dispatcher, so
that the key handling would sit behind the isolation boundary such code belongs
behind. It works. It is also unnecessary, because BL31 can do the transfer itself,
and that is what ships.

It stays here because it cost real findings that are worth keeping:

  - a blanket `MT_MEMORY | MT_RW` mapping is execute-never, so the payload died
    silently on its first instruction after enabling the MMU. Then it died inside
    `mmap_add_region` even section by section - which is why it now runs with the
    MMU off, on purpose.
  - the generic timer is unusable at S-EL1 here: a task waited on a deadline that
    never arrived. Every loop in it is counted, not timed.
  - the dispatcher answers `SMC_RET3(x1, x2, x3)`, so the normal world sees a0 = 0
    and the verdict in a1. Our own SiP answers `SMC_RET1`, verdict in a0. Mixing
    those two up once made a working chain look like a refusal.

Not in any release: the sources build only with `SPD=tspd`, and the artefact gate in
the main repository refuses an image that carries them. The honest trade-off of
shipping the EL3 route instead is written down where it belongs: our HDCP code runs
at the highest privilege level, and a bug in it is an EL3 bug.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
